#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""steward-signals: the helper for a topic steward, which reads its mailbox and its files, keeps its daily metrics file, and checks them.

A topic steward is an agent-deck conductor that watches one agent-mail channel. This helper never opens a
message file, never writes to the mailbox and never sends anything, and it reads metadata only, with one exception: the probe (below).
The one thing it writes is the steward's daily metrics file (below).

POLICY.md holds the steward's thresholds and self-caps as a Markdown table, | key | value | meaning |, with
one row for each key in POLICY_KEYS. A missing, unknown or repeated key, or a value its key cannot use (a
number above zero of at most 12 ASCII digits, a whole number for the six keys that count things; for
summary_local_time a time of day, HH:MM; for summary_tz a tz database name), is refused with the key named;
nothing falls back to a default.

TOPIC.md lists the topic's members as a Markdown table, | name | host | harness |, under a "## Members" heading. A
name that is not an agent-mail name or is listed twice, or a member with no host, is refused with the name shown, and
so is a file with no such heading. Each member's agent-deck row comes from one read-only listing for each host; a host
that does not answer leaves its members unknown, never stopped.

The census lists names and ctimes only: the message files named in the mailbox's top level and in +CHANNEL/, never a message's text.
A file counts only as agent-mail's reader counts it: a regular file, not a symlink or a directory, whose whole name is a message's,
TO_UTC_from-FROM_HEX8.txt, with names of at most 64 characters that do not end in a hyphen and a stamp that is a time. Names are
compared whole, so pi is never pipeline. With the steward's SENT.log (two phases: `intent <seq> <UTC> <to> <subject> <body-sha>`
before each send, `sent <seq> <id>` after) and NOTES.log (`<UTC> <id>` for each direct mail a NOTE named) it shows each member's
unread mail and the age of its oldest, the steward's own NUDGEs still unread, the files in the steward's name that SENT.log does not
account for (no sent line names the file, and no intent that has no sent line yet names the same recipient within 10 seconds of the
stamp), the mail from one member to another that no NOTE names, and how many posts the channel has had since a time, by ctime.

The probe tells whether a member is waiting on John without any text of the member's entering the steward's context. It asks agent-deck for the
end of the member's last response (`session output NAME --max-tokens 200`, in text form: -q would ignore the cap) and reduces it to a word: yes
when its last non-empty line, less the footer agent-deck puts after a response it cut to the cap, ends with a question mark, no when it does not.
The rule is literal: a question followed by its options, or wrapped in emphasis or quotes, reads no, so the steward takes no and unknown alike as
no evidence of a question, never as proof that the member is not waiting on John. The probe prints that word, or unknown, and nothing else. Only a
member on hera is asked, since no output command for a remote member is verified. Unknown too are a member on another host, one that TOPIC.md does
not list, a command that fails or does not finish in 20 seconds, a cut response whose footer is not the one agent-deck 1.16.34 writes (the seam line
of the cut is there, but the last line is not that footer, so where the response ends cannot be told), and a title that two sessions hold, which
agent-deck refuses, so that the wrong session is never read. A TOPIC.md that cannot be read is refused instead: status 2, a reason on stderr and
nothing on stdout. agent-deck logs the read and keeps the whole text of a response it cut in a file of its own. Run from inside a session, it also
records a read of a whole, uncut response as a result read, which withdraws that session's own inbox notice for the turn, and spools a call to the
comms ledger when the ledger is on. A member is not a child of the steward, which never launches a session or sets a parent, so no notice of the
steward's is there to be withdrawn. The helper keeps none of the text.

The metrics file, CONDUCTOR_DIR/metrics/YYYY-MM-DD.json, is the steward's count of one day: a JSON object that the steward adds to as the day goes. The
metrics command merges what it is given into the file, so that no counter is lost: a whole number keeps the larger of the old and the new, an object is
merged key by key, and any other value, or one of another kind than the old, is replaced by the new. The helper keeps whatever keys it is given. The new
file is written whole to a temporary file in metrics/ and moved over the old one, so a reader never sees half of it. A file that is not a JSON object is
moved aside to YYYY-MM-DD.json.bad, with one notice on stderr, and a fresh one is written.

Commands:
  self-test [SECTION ...]
      Run the built-in checks, each section in a fresh temporary home.
  probe NAME --dir CONDUCTOR_DIR
      Print yes, no or unknown: whether the last response of the member NAME ends with a question. The member's host is read from the
      Members table of the TOPIC.md in CONDUCTOR_DIR. A TOPIC.md that cannot be read is refused: status 2, a reason on stderr, nothing on stdout.
  metrics --dir CONDUCTOR_DIR [--date YYYY-MM-DD] --set KEY=VALUE [--set KEY=VALUE ...]
      Merge counters into CONDUCTOR_DIR/metrics/DATE.json, DATE being today's local date unless --date gives one, and print the file's path. A value
      of at most 12 digits, with a minus sign or none, is stored as a whole number and any other value as text; a key with dots, interventions.nudge,
      puts its value in an object. A --set with no =, a key that is not lower-case words joined by dots, a key given twice or used both as a value and
      as the start of another key, a DATE that is not a day, and a CONDUCTOR_DIR that does not exist are refused: exit status 2, a reason on stderr, nothing written.
"""

